WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.114
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.114 --> 00:00:05.780
ANKUR KOTWAL: Good
afternoon, everybody.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.690
How's everyone doing?

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:08.694
Yeah?

00:00:08.694 --> 00:00:11.110
Well, I hope you're having a
good time here at Cloud Next.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:14.290
My name is Ankur Kotwal,
and I'm a developer advocate

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:17.450
on the Google Maps API team.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.520
Today I want to talk
to you about how

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.540
you can build with the Google
Maps APIs for your use cases,

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:27.620
for your industries, and how the
Google Maps APIs fit together

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:30.300
to provide great solutions.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.470
But before we get too
far down that line,

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:36.700
I actually want to take a
quick trip down memory lane.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.910
The Google Maps APIs is
one of the first APIs

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:41.630
that Google ever launched.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:45.350
And it happened actually
a bit over 11 years ago.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.350
That's when we first
launched the APIs.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:51.940
And the consumer product, the
actual Google Maps website,

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.300
hadn't launched much
earlier than that.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.180
When we launched the website
itself, the consumer product,

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.190
it was the first time that we
were transitioning paper maps

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:04.569
to digital maps.

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:07.780
Now, if you think
back 11-12 years ago,

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.800
it's not that long ago.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:14.050
But this idea today of us using
paper maps to get anywhere

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:15.130
seems quite archaic.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:15.700
We've moved.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.250
Our behaviors have changed
substantially since then,

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:21.670
where you'd pull up this
big folding paper map,

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:25.210
try and find your current
location in some index, which

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.000
would give you a page number
maybe or a grid number.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.080
You'd look it up.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.060
And then if you actually
had to go somewhere,

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.671
then you had to find
your destination

00:01:32.671 --> 00:01:34.045
and you had to
route it yourself.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.270
Today, you would pretty
much never do that.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:44.590
So as we started to build
our Google Maps apps,

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:47.350
we started with the base map.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:49.600
And we started to then add
satellite imagery as well,

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:50.433
make that available.

00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:53.980
Suddenly we were able to see
the real world from space,

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.990
effectively, what you
can see from space.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:58.270
We added some
elevation and terrain.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:01.030
But it was really when
smartphones came along

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:04.720
that we hit this inflection
point of usability.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.220
Suddenly we had the
world in our pocket.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.199
We had maps in our pockets.

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:12.370
We were able to harness the
power of these smartphones

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:15.010
to include things
like 3D buildings.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.044
We were actually able
to direct people.

00:02:17.044 --> 00:02:18.460
They no longer
needed to do things

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:22.000
themselves to get places.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.670
And of course, places, I
used that word just now.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:26.860
We were able to
now start to bring

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.850
semantic meaning to where you
are or where you were going.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:35.170
You didn't have to think about
your destination as an address.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:39.800
And that's immensely powerful,
and I'll show you why.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.720
And then the world we
live in today, we're

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.570
very demanding of
our mapping products.

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:48.500
We expect that when
we're routing somewhere,

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.480
when we're heading
to work, let's

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.925
say, that if there is
an incident on the road,

00:02:52.925 --> 00:02:55.550
if there are some roadworks, if
there's an accident, if there's

00:02:55.550 --> 00:03:00.080
a slowdown that we expect our
apps will actually route us

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:01.370
around there.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:04.010
Think about doing that
in the paper days.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.380
You'd just kind
of try and route,

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:07.880
follow the route
that you'd set out,

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.200
and hope that you
made it on time,

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.100
let alone try to
dynamically route yourself

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.440
around any issues
that you came across.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.050
And we also have
things like transit.

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:19.820
We're starting to surface
information about foot traffic

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:21.700
as well.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:25.620
But the cool thing is this was
our story for the consumer app.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:29.640
We've actually followed the
same story for our APIs.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.790
Because we believe
that developers

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:35.430
are key to this story, that
we're not the only ones

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.080
that can build
magical experiences

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.710
with this kind of information.

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:42.870
And it's the Google Maps
APIs that are powering apps

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:45.360
with this kind of information.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:48.480
And when you look at
our suite of APIs,

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.160
you can then start to see that
those APIs can combine together

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.570
to address some really
interesting use cases.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:58.170
Fairly straightforward
use cases,

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:00.170
like store locators,
which you might think

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:01.880
is like a pin on a map.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:05.450
But store locators can be
so much more than that.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:07.820
Real estate agencies
use store locators

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:12.830
or a version of store
locators, to show their users

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:14.900
what their dream homes
are going to look like,

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.810
what area they're going to
be, how close the beach is.

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:19.620
Put on satellite imagery
for a beach house

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:23.610
and you've sold the
place very quickly.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:28.260
And consider things
like ride-sharing today,

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:29.730
or ride-hailing.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.490
In the old days, we would stand
on the side of the street,

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:36.030
stick out a hand, hope that
a taxi or a cab went past

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:37.980
and was available.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.540
And we would then be on our way.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:44.550
Today, we pull
out a phone, we're

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:48.300
able to then say, hey,
find me my closest taxi.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.017
It will find a driver for you.

00:04:50.017 --> 00:04:52.350
But if you think about the
other side of the experience,

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.360
where you actually tell
it your destination,

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.560
you don't have to type in
a full address anymore.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.476
You don't have to swipe
your map to get there.

00:04:58.476 --> 00:05:00.720
You type in a few characters,
and it autocompletes

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.100
what your destination is.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.064
These apps are smart
enough to remember

00:05:04.064 --> 00:05:05.730
what your most popular
destinations are,

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:07.688
where you came from, and
where you're going to.

00:05:07.688 --> 00:05:10.630
And the Maps APIs are enabling
this great user experience.

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:13.920
They're enabling this transition
or this transformation

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.400
in user behaviors.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:17.890
So look at those
use cases, and we

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:20.080
start to see these
industries that these use

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:23.350
cases can have impact and
have positive impact on.

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:26.890
We just talked about
transportation and logistics.

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:27.850
Think about insurance.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:30.670
We're able to see the
kinds of foot traffic

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:32.040
that you get in certain areas.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:35.710
So if you park your car out
on the streets in a busy area,

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:37.330
that insurance premium
could be higher

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:40.330
than if you happen to have
a garage in the same area.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:42.460
And these industries,
new industries,

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:45.050
are sprouting up all the time.

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:47.210
If I had given this
talk a year ago,

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:50.650
I would not have included
gaming on this slide.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.870
Not because gaming
is a new industry,

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:59.110
but in the last nine months we
have seen Pokemon Go explode.

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:02.220
And it's because that team,
that brilliant team at Niantic,

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:03.610
were able to work
out how to make

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:07.060
location-based gaming
or real-world gaming

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:10.291
relevant to all these users.

00:06:10.291 --> 00:06:12.040
And one of the data
sources that they used

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:14.050
was our Google Maps APIs.

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:18.830
So, you can see that these APIs
are really powering big things.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:22.470
Which brings me
to today's topic,

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:24.460
which is the Maps API layers.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:28.040
I'm going to present to you
four layers for developing

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:29.327
on the Google Maps APIs.

00:06:29.327 --> 00:06:31.160
And the way I'm going
to present this to you

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:34.370
is talk to you about
use cases and industries

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:38.240
that we're able to address
with each of these.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:43.100
Now think of this
stack as a developers

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:44.630
stack, where you
start at the top.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:47.480
There's minimal code
required from your side.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.580
But as we go further
down the stack,

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:52.760
we get increased
customizability and control

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:55.940
of how we present
our experience.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:57.980
So let's start with delegation.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:01.580
And delegation really is
about saying, you know what.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:05.750
I believe I need some sort of
mapping, some sort of location

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:06.964
intelligence in my app.

00:07:06.964 --> 00:07:09.130
But I'm going to let somebody
else handle it for me.

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:11.510
I'm going to let Google
handle it for me.

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:13.970
For whatever reason, it could
be for development constraint

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:16.790
reasons, or it might
just not be a core piece

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:18.940
of functionality in your app.

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:22.850
And on Android, we
have this mechanism

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:26.300
called Intents, where you
can from one app intent out

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:27.650
to another app.

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:29.990
And you can provide
some additional context

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:32.780
so that it can initialize
that other app to whatever it

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:34.190
is that you're looking for.

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:36.150
So if you look at
these intents here,

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:38.820
the first thing we're doing is
loading a map of San Francisco.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.490
So this is where,
in your app, you

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:42.230
might want to send your users.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:46.400
You want to provide them a
map of some sort, centered

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:47.360
around San Francisco.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:49.620
And in three lines of
code, you can say, hey,

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:52.950
give me that latitude-longitude
which is line one.

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:56.720
We create that URI, create an
intent passing that URI in.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:58.534
And then we set this
package that says,

00:07:58.534 --> 00:08:00.200
hey, send it off to
Maps, and then start

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:03.860
activity, four lines of code.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:06.095
We can also do it for
things like actual searches.

00:08:06.095 --> 00:08:07.970
So maybe we don't know
the actual destination

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:10.940
we want to go and we're
just doing some discovery.

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:13.550
So here we put in the lat
long that we talked about

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:14.180
at the start.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:17.870
But we also added a
query for restaurants.

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:19.940
We can kick off navigation.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:24.260
Because many apps kind
of want to point users

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:26.450
to their destination, but
not necessarily control

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:27.890
that navigation experience.

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:28.590
We can do that.

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:32.549
And here we're going to
Taronga Zoo in Australia.

00:08:32.549 --> 00:08:35.210
We can even fire up Street View.

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:36.710
Now we've talked
about Android here.

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:39.419
But you can achieve
the same thing on iOS.

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:44.580
And on iOS, the comparable
feature is called URL Scheme.

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:46.290
So if you look at
the demo on the left,

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:47.490
we're just hitting launch.

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:50.014
And it's actually
firing up the browser.

00:08:50.014 --> 00:08:51.930
Now what I'm going to
show you is the iOS code

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:53.220
to achieve this.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:55.440
And the reason that
there's more code on iOS,

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:58.420
is one, it's Objective-C. So
it's a little bit more verbose.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:01.440
But two, that first if
condition that block,

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:05.160
is checking whether the user has
the Google Maps app installed.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.060
If it does, it's
going to use it.

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:09.060
So on Android, you can
be fairly confident

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:10.990
that users are going
to have Google Maps.

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:13.551
On iOS it's sort of a
personal preference.

00:09:13.551 --> 00:09:14.550
Some users will have it.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:15.660
Many users will have it.

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:16.780
Some users won't.

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:20.164
And so a piece of code like
this, in the first if block,

00:09:20.164 --> 00:09:22.080
we're able to check,
hey, do you have the app.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:23.760
If so, fire up Google Maps.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:25.980
If not, just fall
back to the browser,

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:28.940
and we're able to
accomplish the same things.

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:29.920
So that's simple.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:31.630
That's that first
layer of delegation,

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:34.960
we're not really doing
anything inside of our apps.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:37.670
We're letting Google
handle it for us.

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:41.220
Layer two of this
stack is incorporation,

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:43.810
where we want to put a little
bit of mapping functionality

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:48.130
in, but again, it's fairly
light functionality.

00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:50.120
We just want a simple map.

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:55.060
And here on a website, you
could use the Static Maps API.

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:56.905
The cool thing about
the Static Maps API

00:09:56.905 --> 00:09:59.920
is it's just a URL
that you pass in.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:01.890
And you put in the
parameters that you need.

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:04.750
So we've said the center
is a particular location.

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:06.490
We specify the zoom levels.

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:09.680
We can even specify a
resolution in our API key.

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:11.950
And this will return
back us an image.

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:15.310
So the nice thing about this is
there's no JavaScript involved.

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:18.370
You just put an image tag
with this URL around it,

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:20.290
and you're away.

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:22.375
We give you some
customization options as well.

00:10:22.375 --> 00:10:24.250
So as you can see from
the three screenshots,

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:27.100
we've applied a custom style
on the very left-hand side.

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:28.600
It's a monochrome style.

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:32.050
On the second slide, we've
used satellite imagery.

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:34.675
And on the third one,
we've actually drawn a line

00:10:34.675 --> 00:10:36.550
that there's a blue line
in the middle that's

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:39.210
navigating you from--

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:41.050
I think in this case,
it's my hotel room

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:44.080
to my office in Mountain View.

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:48.810
But if you want to go
beyond Static Maps,

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:50.510
if you want to make
it interactive,

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:52.980
if you don't want just a
picture, we have the Embed API.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:56.560
And that allows you to
embed a map onto your site.

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:59.280
It'll put it in a nice
little frame there,

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.680
and you're doing it by
specifying an I-frame.

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:05.070
And you can do things like,
again, provide directions

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:06.660
or even do street view.

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:08.970
So we're just starting to
touch the surface of some

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:11.804
of the mapping
functionality we can do.

00:11:11.804 --> 00:11:13.470
Now for the next piece
of incorporation,

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:15.660
I'm going to look at
a specific use case.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:18.220
And that's location sharing.

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:20.650
Now it may not be
obvious to everybody,

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:24.760
but location sharing is actually
something we do very often.

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:28.480
And it's useful in
many, many contexts.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:31.104
It might be as simple as just
letting your family member know

00:11:31.104 --> 00:11:32.770
that you're running
late, because you're

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:33.850
stuck in traffic.

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:35.808
You might location share,
I'm stuck in traffic.

00:11:35.808 --> 00:11:37.480
I'm here.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:41.620
It could be that you're saying
I need to get somewhere.

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:44.980
So going back to our
ride-sharing application

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:47.620
where we're trying to
find a taxi for us,

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:49.650
we usually share
our current location

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:51.670
so we can get picked up there.

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:53.350
Now that might seem
like a lot of work.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:55.720
But we've tried to make
it really, really easy;

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:59.624
easy for you as developers
and easy for your users.

00:11:59.624 --> 00:12:01.790
And there's two things I'm
going to talk about here.

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:03.640
The first one is
the Place Picker.

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:07.000
And the Place Picker
is a UI widget

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:09.190
that you can just
drop into your apps

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:11.420
and it provides this
fantastic user experience.

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:13.300
I'm going to kick
off the animation.

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:15.910
But you have a
map in the middle.

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:18.670
You can drag it around,
you can drop it on a pin.

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:20.629
And then below that
map, we actually

00:12:20.629 --> 00:12:21.670
have a list of locations.

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:24.220
So in case you don't want
to interact with the map,

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:26.320
you can just pull up
the list of locations

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.540
and identify which
place you're at.

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:30.250
There is also a
search box at the top,

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:32.890
so you can type in which
location you're at.

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:35.380
But the nice thing is we
provide an out-of-the-box user

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:38.860
experience that's very familiar
to users and is powered

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:40.330
by the Google Maps APIs.

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:44.680
So you know it's going to be
accurate and you can trust it.

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:47.740
It's an easy way to have your
users share their location.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:50.410
Because they can just
pick what's there.

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:53.810
The nice thing is it
requires very little code.

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:57.250
So on Android, we have
two parts to this code.

00:12:57.250 --> 00:13:00.021
The first part is we're going
to start off the Place Picker

00:13:00.021 --> 00:13:00.520
activity.

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:02.686
We're going to say, hey,
go ahead and show the Place

00:13:02.686 --> 00:13:03.310
Picker.

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:06.850
We create one of these Place
Picker intent builders.

00:13:06.850 --> 00:13:10.570
And then we call this method
startActivityForResult.

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:12.160
And the reason we
call that method

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:14.890
is because we want to know when
the user has finished selecting

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:17.510
a place that that
process has finished

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:19.690
and it can pass us the result.

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:23.320
So once the user goes and
interacts with this widget,

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:24.480
we get a call back.

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:27.585
We just confirm that we've
come back because of the Place

00:13:27.585 --> 00:13:29.290
Picker, and then
we get the place

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:33.010
that the user selected,
couldn't be easier.

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:34.780
And suddenly location
sharing, which can

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:36.490
be a hugely complex affair--

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:38.740
can you imagine trying to
code an experience like this

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:41.560
dropping a map in,
having scrollable lists,

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:44.950
using GPS or Wi-Fi to
actually populate those lists

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:46.480
in a meaningful way?

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.580
It's not necessarily
trivial code.

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:51.490
But we've trivialized it by
providing this out-of-the-box

00:13:51.490 --> 00:13:52.600
widget.

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:55.130
And we have parity
on iOS as well.

00:13:55.130 --> 00:13:57.700
So on iOS, again, it's a
similar amount of code.

00:13:57.700 --> 00:14:01.030
We're going to instantiate
a GMSPlacePickerConfig.

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:02.980
We're going to go
ahead and kick it off,

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:04.660
so it displays the Place Picker.

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:06.160
And when we get the
call back, we're

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:07.660
just going to pull that place.

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:09.205
You see that last
if condition there.

00:14:09.205 --> 00:14:11.080
We're just going to pull
the place out of it.

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:14.466
And in this case, we're
getting the place names.

00:14:14.466 --> 00:14:16.140
That's it.

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:18.250
That's how easy it
is to share location.

00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:21.480
Another example where our
ride-sharing application

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:25.840
might be useful is
something autocomplete.

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:27.540
So if you think
about ride-sharing,

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:30.340
when we want to get picked up,
we always have a place to go.

00:14:30.340 --> 00:14:33.360
So if you think about
your ride-sharing app,

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:35.310
you look up at your destination.

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:38.150
You know, I'm not a fan of the
keyboard on mobile devices.

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:39.690
So I don't like to type a lot.

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:41.000
It's not easy to do.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:43.060
It's easy to make
typos, and so on.

00:14:43.060 --> 00:14:46.110
Autocomplete here is
powered by the same magic

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:48.570
that powers Google
search and Google Maps

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:52.200
in terms of its recognizing
what your intent is

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:53.790
with few characters.

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:57.360
So an autocomplete widget
makes a lot of sense

00:14:57.360 --> 00:14:58.830
whenever you need
to ask the user

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:01.710
for an address of any sort.

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:02.910
They can look by name.

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:05.139
They can look by its address.

00:15:05.139 --> 00:15:06.930
If they really want
to, they could probably

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:08.500
look by latitude-longitude
as well.

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:11.150
But really, humans
don't work in that way.

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:15.690
Anyways, on iOS, having
an autocomplete widget

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:18.090
the amount of code is
similar to the Place Picker.

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:21.110
We are creating one of these
autocomplete view controllers.

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:22.890
We're firing it off.

00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:25.980
We get a callback at the
bottom there with the place

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:27.270
that the user selected.

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:29.530
And we're able to
pull the name out.

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:32.200
On Android, again the
code looks very similar.

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:35.760
We have an intent for
the place autocomplete.

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:38.620
We go ahead and call
startActivityForResult,

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:40.480
and we get the result back.

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:43.990
So location sharing becomes
simple, yet very refined

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:46.150
as a user experience.

00:15:46.150 --> 00:15:52.410
So that's some examples
of incorporation.

00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:54.680
But I know most of the
developers I work with,

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:56.420
are like, I really
want to go deeper.

00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:58.560
I want to be able to
write some code myself

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:00.470
and produce some magic.

00:16:00.470 --> 00:16:02.690
And that's really what
integration, layer three,

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:03.530
is about.

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:05.490
It's about saying,
hey, you know what?

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:08.120
This Maps API, I want to use it.

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:09.410
I want the data from it.

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:10.430
I want to customize it.

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:12.110
I want to really make it my own.

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:14.310
I want to personalize it.

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:17.380
And the use case where this
becomes immensely useful

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:19.900
is when you want
to visualize a map.

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:25.000
So let me show you this
particular demo here.

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:27.340
Well, it's a screenshot.

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:30.900
So Google I/O is our other
large conference for developers,

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:32.420
and we have that annually.

00:16:32.420 --> 00:16:35.860
And in 2016, Google
I/O for the first time

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:39.820
was actually held at our
campus in Mountain View.

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:42.580
Now Mountain View is actually
quite far from the main San

00:16:42.580 --> 00:16:43.712
Francisco city.

00:16:43.712 --> 00:16:45.670
And we knew that there
would be a lot of people

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:47.628
that would be traveling
from all over the world

00:16:47.628 --> 00:16:49.090
to attend this conference.

00:16:49.090 --> 00:16:52.360
So what we did is that
we organized buses

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:55.570
to pick up these attendees
from various hotels

00:16:55.570 --> 00:16:57.640
and various parts of
San Francisco city,

00:16:57.640 --> 00:17:00.710
and actually all
around the Bay Area.

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:04.030
And these buses would
then take these attendees

00:17:04.030 --> 00:17:06.230
to the conference and back.

00:17:06.230 --> 00:17:08.770
But we thought what
would be great is

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:11.950
if we could show a demonstration
of our Google Maps APIs,

00:17:11.950 --> 00:17:16.180
and display on a
dashboard our bus tracker.

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:17.740
So we built a bus tracker.

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:19.599
And if you look at
this, this layer 3

00:17:19.599 --> 00:17:22.119
of integration, what
we're doing here

00:17:22.119 --> 00:17:26.500
is that we've grabbed the
usual standard JS Maps API v3,

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:28.420
but we've custom styled it.

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:30.040
We've put custom
markers on there

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:33.010
to represent buses
and represent hotels.

00:17:33.010 --> 00:17:37.750
We're using polygons to draw the
path that the buses are taking.

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:40.720
And then we have some just
standard HTML elements

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:43.900
that sit on top that tell you
where these buses are going,

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:47.460
which stops, which hotels
it's going to be stopping at.

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:50.260
But we also added
time estimates.

00:17:50.260 --> 00:17:54.480
So if you look at the top of
the boxes, it says leaving in,

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:56.747
next in, and then on
the left-hand side

00:17:56.747 --> 00:17:58.830
it tells you time estimates
of how long it's going

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:01.110
to take to get to those spots.

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:02.580
And we're using
the Directions API

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:03.905
to get those time estimates.

00:18:03.905 --> 00:18:06.030
But I'll talk about the
Directions API a little bit

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:07.820
later.

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:10.190
But here is an example
of integration,

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:13.130
where we've really changed
the visualization of how

00:18:13.130 --> 00:18:14.290
these maps are.

00:18:14.290 --> 00:18:16.710
Now if you're interested
in an example like this,

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:19.980
we actually open sourced this
only a couple of weeks ago.

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:23.000
So if you go to
github.com/googlemaps,

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:25.880
and look for transport tracker,
you can see the full source

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:31.570
code for this, and use that
for your purposes as well.

00:18:31.570 --> 00:18:34.580
Now we've had custom
styling on maps

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:38.530
for the web version, for the
JS API, for a number of years.

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:41.460
And one of the biggest requests
that we've had from developers

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:43.956
is when is it coming
to Android and iOS.

00:18:43.956 --> 00:18:45.330
So it was only a
couple of months

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:49.410
ago that we were thrilled
to be able to launch Custom

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:51.960
Styling for Android and iOS.

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:55.530
And as you can see from the
animated screenshot here,

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:57.630
it works exactly
as you'd expect.

00:18:57.630 --> 00:19:01.460
You can change the
style at runtime.

00:19:01.460 --> 00:19:04.220
And you don't need to tear the
map down and bring it back up.

00:19:04.220 --> 00:19:07.610
You just call setStyle, and
it goes ahead and applies it.

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:09.650
So examples of where
this could be useful

00:19:09.650 --> 00:19:12.440
is if you're building a fitness
app, or some sort of a driving

00:19:12.440 --> 00:19:14.620
app or navigation app.

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:19.240
If you change the style to
reflect the time of day,

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:22.150
so as it hits evening time we're
able to switch into a night

00:19:22.150 --> 00:19:25.092
mode theme and vice versa.

00:19:25.092 --> 00:19:26.800
The other really cool
thing about the way

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:31.000
we did custom styling is
that you specify your styles

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:33.370
using JSON file.

00:19:33.370 --> 00:19:35.330
And we provided
a styling wizard.

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:38.510
So if you just do a Google
search for Google Maps Styling

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:43.240
Wizard, you'll get to a site
where you can see a map.

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:45.610
And you get a bunch of really
friendly sliders and radio

00:19:45.610 --> 00:19:49.300
buttons, where you can get
custom style your map for you.

00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:51.730
And it can export a JSON file.

00:19:51.730 --> 00:19:53.410
Now the nice thing
about that JSON file,

00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:55.120
is it's the same
format whether you

00:19:55.120 --> 00:19:57.490
want to use custom
styling on the web,

00:19:57.490 --> 00:20:00.850
on Android, or on
iOS, a single file.

00:20:00.850 --> 00:20:04.120
And you can either ship
that JSON file on your app

00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:07.490
or website, or you
can host it yourself

00:20:07.490 --> 00:20:09.590
and then at runtime
dynamically pull down

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:11.330
whatever is your current style.

00:20:11.330 --> 00:20:14.780
So, we give you full
flexibility there.

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:16.400
It's not just about
changing colors.

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:18.800
You can actually turn
on or off landmarks.

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:20.990
You can change the
density of labels.

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:23.630
So maybe you don't care for
points of interest in your app,

00:20:23.630 --> 00:20:25.760
or you don't need to
show as many roads.

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:28.730
We have sliders that enable
you to turn that up or down.

00:20:28.730 --> 00:20:31.220
There's a ton of
flexibility here.

00:20:31.220 --> 00:20:32.990
And this is the
type of integration

00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:35.420
and that attention to
detail in your apps

00:20:35.420 --> 00:20:39.640
that makes your apps
shine and stand out.

00:20:39.640 --> 00:20:42.220
So that's how we look
at the map as a whole.

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:47.160
I want to switch gears and talk
a little bit about the camera.

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:51.390
So if you see here, this
is downtown Los Angeles.

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:52.850
We've just got an Android app.

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:55.640
We've focused on
downtown Los Angeles.

00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:58.100
And this is a style of map
that you're used to seeing.

00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:00.560
In fact, you can see the
heritage of paper maps coming

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:01.770
through here.

00:21:01.770 --> 00:21:06.080
But we're on a digital device,
and realistically if you're,

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:07.850
let's say, building
a real estate app,

00:21:07.850 --> 00:21:10.349
and you want to show people
what their next dream home could

00:21:10.349 --> 00:21:15.430
look like, showing them
some perspective of that map

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:19.460
is a much better user experience
than necessarily showing it

00:21:19.460 --> 00:21:20.720
from top down.

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:24.110
Now having this sort
of depth, 3D buildings,

00:21:24.110 --> 00:21:26.390
really nicely zoomed
in and having tilt,

00:21:26.390 --> 00:21:28.820
that's not necessarily
applicable for every use case.

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:32.840
But you can certainly see having
this sort of rich visualization

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:35.840
in certain types of apps,
like the real estate up,

00:21:35.840 --> 00:21:40.130
add a lot of value
to your users.

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:44.200
And pulling this sort of thing
off is incredibly simple.

00:21:46.940 --> 00:21:50.440
We have, when you specify a
new camera position on Android,

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:53.199
you have to create one of
these camera position objects.

00:21:53.199 --> 00:21:54.740
We have a couple of
methods on there.

00:21:54.740 --> 00:21:57.581
So some of these values you
should recognize, things

00:21:57.581 --> 00:21:59.830
like target, which is the
latitude and longitude where

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:02.860
you want to focus
on; things like zoom,

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:04.510
which is how far you are.

00:22:04.510 --> 00:22:07.870
But then bearing allows you
to turn the camera basically

00:22:07.870 --> 00:22:10.780
like a compass to see which
direction you're facing.

00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:14.580
And tilt is the thing that comes
in and puts some depth on it.

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:17.680
So it brings that camera really
closer down to the surface,

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:19.530
if you choose.

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:21.270
Just adding those
extra parameters--

00:22:21.270 --> 00:22:23.430
and they're just
numeric values--

00:22:23.430 --> 00:22:27.540
adds so much more to the
experience of your app.

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:29.130
And the other thing
that I recommend

00:22:29.130 --> 00:22:32.190
is that as you change
your camera position,

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:33.930
don't just snap the
camera to place.

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:35.250
Don't move the camera.

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:38.400
Use APIs like this one
called Animate Camera.

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:40.620
And what Animate Camera
does is it works out

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:43.260
the difference between
where the camera is looking

00:22:43.260 --> 00:22:45.840
at now compared to
where it needs to be,

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:48.000
and it gracefully
animates the camera there

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:50.460
in a nice smooth flow.

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:52.940
So if your users are going
through, let's say, houses--

00:22:52.940 --> 00:22:54.600
we'll continue
with that example--

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:55.140
in a list.

00:22:55.140 --> 00:22:57.270
And they're selecting
the next one.

00:22:57.270 --> 00:22:58.890
Don't just jump
the camera there.

00:22:58.890 --> 00:22:59.430
Move them.

00:22:59.430 --> 00:23:02.850
Help them retain that context
of where they're moving to

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:05.650
and where they came from.

00:23:05.650 --> 00:23:09.130
On iOS we achieved
the same thing.

00:23:09.130 --> 00:23:12.820
Again with iOS in
Objective-C, we

00:23:12.820 --> 00:23:15.760
use a GMS camera
position object instead.

00:23:15.760 --> 00:23:17.890
We have at the bottom
of those parameters,

00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:20.320
you can see bearing.

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:23.100
And instead of tilt on iOS,
it's called viewing angle.

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:25.070
But it means exactly
the same thing.

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:26.800
And as you can see
with this demo here,

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:30.230
we have the same sort of
depth, 3D buildings and so on.

00:23:32.950 --> 00:23:36.640
Let's have a look at another
use case, a store locator.

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:39.070
And as I mentioned
earlier, store locators

00:23:39.070 --> 00:23:41.860
are much more than
just a marker on a map.

00:23:41.860 --> 00:23:44.530
You can make them
very rich and useful,

00:23:44.530 --> 00:23:48.780
beyond just one
particular point.

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:50.120
So here's what I did.

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:52.070
I was a little bit
naive, and I searched

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:54.830
for Italian restaurants in Rome.

00:23:54.830 --> 00:23:57.620
And I managed to get
quite a few results here.

00:23:57.620 --> 00:24:00.660
It shouldn't be a
surprise to anybody.

00:24:00.660 --> 00:24:03.810
Just dropping markers where all
these Italian restaurants are,

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:05.920
this is not a great
user experience.

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:08.310
Imagine trying to make
sense of this like, hey,

00:24:08.310 --> 00:24:11.700
I'm visiting Rome, and
where should I go and eat?

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:15.150
There's really not much
to be gained from here.

00:24:15.150 --> 00:24:17.430
A better way to present
this is with something

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:19.440
that we call marker clustering.

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:21.565
And this is what marker
clustering looks like.

00:24:21.565 --> 00:24:23.190
You'll see there's
some clusters there.

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:26.420
And as we zoom in, those
clusters split apart,

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:28.170
and we're able to see
more concentrations

00:24:28.170 --> 00:24:29.190
of these markers.

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:31.920
But as we zoom out and
they start to overlap,

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:33.090
we coalesce them.

00:24:33.090 --> 00:24:35.760
They group up into
these clusters.

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:37.770
And visually we put
a count on there,

00:24:37.770 --> 00:24:42.260
so that your user can see that
this is an important cluster

00:24:42.260 --> 00:24:42.760
or not.

00:24:42.760 --> 00:24:44.676
So we actually change
the size of the cluster.

00:24:44.676 --> 00:24:46.525
We put numbers
around it, and so on.

00:24:46.525 --> 00:24:48.150
This is part of an
open source library,

00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:51.220
and I'll tell you about
that library in a second.

00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:52.790
So you can customize
all of this.

00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:54.165
But let me show
you how much code

00:24:54.165 --> 00:24:57.840
is required to implement
marker clustering in your apps.

00:24:57.840 --> 00:24:59.400
The first thing
you need to do is

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:01.570
tell in your gradle
configuration,

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:06.410
specify that you want to use
the Android Maps utils library.

00:25:06.410 --> 00:25:10.224
The second thing you need to
do is look at your data class.

00:25:10.224 --> 00:25:11.890
And in our case, our
data class is going

00:25:11.890 --> 00:25:13.240
to be our list of restaurants.

00:25:13.240 --> 00:25:16.060
So we'll have restaurants
with name and lat long

00:25:16.060 --> 00:25:16.810
and whatever else.

00:25:16.810 --> 00:25:21.700
Maybe it'll be rating,
cost, any number of things.

00:25:21.700 --> 00:25:23.672
But all we need to
do is add this one--

00:25:23.672 --> 00:25:25.630
implement this interface,
this one extra method

00:25:25.630 --> 00:25:29.530
called getPosition, which just
returns us latitude longitude.

00:25:29.530 --> 00:25:32.620
Once we have that done,
we create or instantiate

00:25:32.620 --> 00:25:35.440
one of these cluster
managers and we tell our map

00:25:35.440 --> 00:25:41.154
that any time the camera changes
or the user clicks on the map

00:25:41.154 --> 00:25:43.570
that the map should let the
Cluster Manager know about it.

00:25:43.570 --> 00:25:45.610
And that's how when we
interact with the map,

00:25:45.610 --> 00:25:49.000
those clusters are coalescing
and forming new ones,

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:50.800
or splitting up.

00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:54.490
And then step four, we go ahead
and tell our Cluster Manager

00:25:54.490 --> 00:25:57.040
that here are all
our restaurants.

00:25:57.040 --> 00:25:58.680
I didn't put the
step five on there.

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:01.013
But the step five is that you
should go ahead and remove

00:26:01.013 --> 00:26:04.520
any code that you have, to
add markers onto the map,

00:26:04.520 --> 00:26:06.027
or take marketers
out of the map.

00:26:06.027 --> 00:26:08.360
Because the Cluster Manager
is going to do that for you.

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:11.000
So in these 10 to
15 lines of code,

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:15.930
we've suddenly implemented
marker clustering,

00:26:15.930 --> 00:26:17.910
and we've probably
reduced the amount of code

00:26:17.910 --> 00:26:19.860
that we had from
before we started,

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:22.290
so a great user experience
with minimal effort

00:26:22.290 --> 00:26:25.720
from a development standpoint.

00:26:25.720 --> 00:26:28.860
Instead of clusters, we
can also do heatmaps.

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:30.360
And the heatmap
implementation looks

00:26:30.360 --> 00:26:33.640
similar to clusters, where
we generate these heatmaps

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:35.200
depending on zoom levels.

00:26:35.200 --> 00:26:38.750
And so as we zoom
in more and more,

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:41.550
you start to see the heatmap
start to split apart.

00:26:41.550 --> 00:26:45.040
Or when we zoom out, it
combines into larger ones.

00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:47.080
Now I mentioned this was
an open source library.

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:48.820
So if you don't like
my alarming colors,

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:50.830
you can definitely change that.

00:26:50.830 --> 00:26:53.710
The API itself allows you
to change that as well.

00:26:53.710 --> 00:26:56.240
But it's very easy to do.

00:26:56.240 --> 00:26:58.480
Again, the implementation
for this is trivial.

00:26:58.480 --> 00:27:00.400
It's the same
library that we use.

00:27:00.400 --> 00:27:04.150
We add that interface we
have on our data items.

00:27:04.150 --> 00:27:07.360
We then instantiate a
HeatmapTileProvider.

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:09.760
This is the thing that's
going to generate the tiles,

00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:14.200
depending on zoom
levels lat longs.

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:17.890
On that step two, I just
want to point out the .data.

00:27:17.890 --> 00:27:20.230
That's where we add
our restaurants.

00:27:20.230 --> 00:27:21.270
It's just that one line.

00:27:21.270 --> 00:27:22.750
It's going to manage it for us.

00:27:22.750 --> 00:27:25.660
And then step three,
we add a tile overlay.

00:27:25.660 --> 00:27:27.580
And we add it to the map itself.

00:27:27.580 --> 00:27:29.110
That's it.

00:27:29.110 --> 00:27:31.510
That's the whole code for
the heatmap implementation.

00:27:31.510 --> 00:27:34.480
So, when it comes to
doing deep integration,

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:37.050
really look at things
like custom styling.

00:27:37.050 --> 00:27:38.350
Personalize your maps.

00:27:38.350 --> 00:27:39.970
Look at ways that
you can uniquely

00:27:39.970 --> 00:27:43.210
present whatever data it is that
you want to present on a map.

00:27:43.210 --> 00:27:44.890
Don't overwhelm your users.

00:27:44.890 --> 00:27:48.220
Use the visual metaphors
where possible.

00:27:48.220 --> 00:27:49.294
All right.

00:27:49.294 --> 00:27:50.710
So we've gotten
to the point where

00:27:50.710 --> 00:27:53.200
we've done some integration.

00:27:53.200 --> 00:27:55.130
But I've had developers
come and tell me,

00:27:55.130 --> 00:27:57.460
but Ankur, I just
want the raw data.

00:27:57.460 --> 00:27:58.120
You know what?

00:27:58.120 --> 00:27:59.434
I will manage everything.

00:27:59.434 --> 00:28:00.100
I want to do it.

00:28:00.100 --> 00:28:01.308
I want to control everything.

00:28:01.308 --> 00:28:05.610
I want to be the
orchestrator of all of this.

00:28:05.610 --> 00:28:10.534
So let's look at an example
in fleet and asset tracking.

00:28:10.534 --> 00:28:12.200
Typically with fleet
and asset tracking,

00:28:12.200 --> 00:28:14.040
we have a number of vehicles.

00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:15.510
We need to do some deliveries.

00:28:15.510 --> 00:28:17.200
We need to optimize around that.

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:20.170
So there's a number of APIs
that can really help here.

00:28:20.170 --> 00:28:22.670
The first is the Directions API.

00:28:22.670 --> 00:28:25.740
Now the Directions
API is a web service.

00:28:25.740 --> 00:28:27.870
So you can call it
on any platform.

00:28:27.870 --> 00:28:32.100
But we do recommend that you
proxy any of our web services

00:28:32.100 --> 00:28:34.410
through a server rather
than consume them directly

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:35.600
on mobile.

00:28:35.600 --> 00:28:37.760
And if you want to know
more about why that is,

00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:41.790
we had a great talk today called
the development best practices

00:28:41.790 --> 00:28:44.070
for Google Maps web services.

00:28:44.070 --> 00:28:46.887
It will be up on YouTube
within a day or so.

00:28:46.887 --> 00:28:47.970
So please do look that up.

00:28:47.970 --> 00:28:51.360
We cover some great
best practices there.

00:28:51.360 --> 00:28:53.640
Back to the Directions
API, the great thing

00:28:53.640 --> 00:28:55.350
about the Directions
API is one, it

00:28:55.350 --> 00:28:58.050
returns you this
nice JSON output.

00:28:58.050 --> 00:29:00.300
It tells you
step-by-step directions

00:29:00.300 --> 00:29:02.100
on how to get somewhere.

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:03.870
It tells you exact
latitude and longitudes

00:29:03.870 --> 00:29:06.304
on how to get your
users to places.

00:29:06.304 --> 00:29:08.970
You can specify different travel
modes, whether you're driving--

00:29:08.970 --> 00:29:12.660
so in the use case we
were talking about,

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:15.300
the asset tracker, it might be
a driving delivery that we're

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:16.940
doing or it might be cycling.

00:29:16.940 --> 00:29:19.470
We could do that too.

00:29:19.470 --> 00:29:22.440
But at the bottom there,
you'll see duration.

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:24.380
We can actually give
you travel times

00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:26.540
of how long it's
going to take for you

00:29:26.540 --> 00:29:28.380
to complete this journey.

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:32.670
And that's immensely powerful,
and very, very useful

00:29:32.670 --> 00:29:34.380
for things like
ride-sharing service

00:29:34.380 --> 00:29:37.380
that we talked about earlier,
or even any delivery service

00:29:37.380 --> 00:29:38.940
that we're going to do.

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:43.470
Now this information is
powered by the same engine

00:29:43.470 --> 00:29:45.990
that powers Google Maps
that does navigation, that

00:29:45.990 --> 00:29:49.504
provides real-time directions.

00:29:49.504 --> 00:29:50.920
We can take this
one step further.

00:29:50.920 --> 00:29:53.430
Let's say our business
isn't actually asking

00:29:53.430 --> 00:29:55.080
for this information right now.

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:57.990
But they care about the delivery
time at 3:00 AM tomorrow

00:29:57.990 --> 00:29:59.220
morning.

00:29:59.220 --> 00:30:01.840
We have predictive
travel times for that.

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:03.960
So what we've done
is over time, we've

00:30:03.960 --> 00:30:07.250
built this hugely
complicated model of traffic

00:30:07.250 --> 00:30:08.670
for all over the world.

00:30:08.670 --> 00:30:11.430
We've put it through our
machine-learning algorithms,

00:30:11.430 --> 00:30:14.190
and developed new
machine-learned models out

00:30:14.190 --> 00:30:14.700
of that.

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:18.850
And we're applying it to
understand traffic conditions,

00:30:18.850 --> 00:30:20.550
not just now but
when they'll will

00:30:20.550 --> 00:30:23.940
be at various times of the week
at various points in the world.

00:30:27.320 --> 00:30:29.890
And of course, if you
know that your vehicle is

00:30:29.890 --> 00:30:31.690
going to be on a
road, you should

00:30:31.690 --> 00:30:34.660
consider using the Roads API.

00:30:34.660 --> 00:30:36.820
Something that actually
surprises a lot of folks,

00:30:36.820 --> 00:30:40.180
is when they're told that
the GPS on their phones,

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:41.920
regardless of which
phone you have,

00:30:41.920 --> 00:30:44.470
it's actually not that accurate.

00:30:44.470 --> 00:30:47.800
There are times-- unless
there's a perfect line of sight,

00:30:47.800 --> 00:30:49.390
it's often not very accurate.

00:30:49.390 --> 00:30:51.700
It gives you a range of values.

00:30:51.700 --> 00:30:54.460
And so even though you're
driving along a straight road,

00:30:54.460 --> 00:30:56.960
you could get a GPS
response up here, up here,

00:30:56.960 --> 00:30:58.690
down there, across there.

00:30:58.690 --> 00:31:00.550
I mean overall you're
going straight,

00:31:00.550 --> 00:31:02.840
and you could start
to see that pattern.

00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:04.930
But the actual data
that it gives you

00:31:04.930 --> 00:31:07.980
is not necessarily straight.

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:10.860
So, I'm going to say
Computer, Enhance.

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:14.860
And we're going to look at
this data that we captured.

00:31:14.860 --> 00:31:17.820
So this is a car that was
traveling around this park

00:31:17.820 --> 00:31:19.380
and around some buildings.

00:31:19.380 --> 00:31:21.930
And what you can see
here is apparently

00:31:21.930 --> 00:31:25.530
this car cut across a park,
drove through the park,

00:31:25.530 --> 00:31:29.830
and further down actually drove
through a building as well.

00:31:29.830 --> 00:31:32.290
Now you can judge my
driving however you want.

00:31:32.290 --> 00:31:35.340
But I did not do that.

00:31:35.340 --> 00:31:37.680
The Roads API can help
us eliminate these sorts

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:40.110
of problems, noisy GPS.

00:31:40.110 --> 00:31:43.930
So what we did is we then
took those exact same points,

00:31:43.930 --> 00:31:46.140
put them through the
Roads API, and we ended up

00:31:46.140 --> 00:31:47.790
with the green line.

00:31:47.790 --> 00:31:51.480
And the Roads API is intelligent
enough to know that, hey look,

00:31:51.480 --> 00:31:55.255
I know that this vehicle was on
a road throughout the journey.

00:31:55.255 --> 00:31:56.880
I'm going to look at
this data, and I'm

00:31:56.880 --> 00:32:01.170
going to interpolate what
is actually happening here.

00:32:01.170 --> 00:32:03.510
And so it will snap
those points to the road.

00:32:03.510 --> 00:32:07.420
But where you've got curved
roads and things as well,

00:32:07.420 --> 00:32:09.930
it will actually
smooth that path out.

00:32:09.930 --> 00:32:13.290
It will add new points to that
list of latitude and longitude,

00:32:13.290 --> 00:32:15.810
so that when you plot it, it
looks nice and smooth rather

00:32:15.810 --> 00:32:18.250
than a jagged polygon.

00:32:18.250 --> 00:32:22.850
So the Roads API is
immensely useful.

00:32:22.850 --> 00:32:26.250
Let's look at local
discovery as a use case.

00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:27.860
Now, in the case
of local discovery,

00:32:27.860 --> 00:32:29.090
we just want to see--

00:32:29.090 --> 00:32:30.950
we want to learn
about other places

00:32:30.950 --> 00:32:33.390
around a particular location.

00:32:33.390 --> 00:32:34.940
So I'm from Sydney, Australia.

00:32:34.940 --> 00:32:36.710
And so when I visit
San Francisco,

00:32:36.710 --> 00:32:38.168
one of the things
that I tend to do

00:32:38.168 --> 00:32:40.490
is, hey, which restaurants
should I go and check out,

00:32:40.490 --> 00:32:44.870
which bars, you know what
attractions there are.

00:32:44.870 --> 00:32:48.250
And one of the APIs where we
provide this information in

00:32:48.250 --> 00:32:50.416
is the Places API.

00:32:50.416 --> 00:32:52.040
And the Places API
is something that we

00:32:52.040 --> 00:32:54.830
provide as a web service,
as well as a native API

00:32:54.830 --> 00:32:56.000
on Android and iOS.

00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:58.750
And I'm going to cover both.

00:32:58.750 --> 00:33:02.110
With the Places API, you
can get lots of information

00:33:02.110 --> 00:33:03.264
about a place.

00:33:03.264 --> 00:33:05.430
Because if you think about
a latitude and longitude,

00:33:05.430 --> 00:33:06.780
it's a point on Earth.

00:33:06.780 --> 00:33:08.530
It doesn't make a lot
of sense when you're

00:33:08.530 --> 00:33:10.087
in a multi-story building.

00:33:10.087 --> 00:33:11.920
In fact, if you're in
a multi-story building

00:33:11.920 --> 00:33:14.170
like the Moscone Center
today, you're not even

00:33:14.170 --> 00:33:15.736
going to get a GPS location.

00:33:15.736 --> 00:33:17.110
Because we don't
have direct line

00:33:17.110 --> 00:33:18.318
of sight with the satellites.

00:33:18.318 --> 00:33:19.720
We're indoors.

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:22.180
We're also not at a
specific-- we're not just

00:33:22.180 --> 00:33:24.010
at a latitude and longitude.

00:33:24.010 --> 00:33:25.390
We have to consider height.

00:33:25.390 --> 00:33:27.310
And this is where
Places can help.

00:33:27.310 --> 00:33:29.920
Places is able to tell
you the difference

00:33:29.920 --> 00:33:34.250
between being on floor three of
this building versus floor two.

00:33:34.250 --> 00:33:35.830
If you're in a
shopping mall, you

00:33:35.830 --> 00:33:38.950
can actually tell whether you're
at a cafe or a hairdresser,

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:42.290
even if they're
somewhat close by.

00:33:42.290 --> 00:33:45.620
And the way it does
this is by using

00:33:45.620 --> 00:33:49.850
information like latitude and
longitudes, Bluetooth beacons,

00:33:49.850 --> 00:33:53.450
Wi-Fi access points, and more,
as well as assisted GPS, which

00:33:53.450 --> 00:33:58.290
is triangulating cell phone
tower information as well.

00:33:58.290 --> 00:34:01.430
But the great thing is
once you have a place,

00:34:01.430 --> 00:34:04.040
it has a ton of
semantic information.

00:34:04.040 --> 00:34:08.080
So here's a response that we
get from the JSON request.

00:34:08.080 --> 00:34:09.580
I've highlighted a
couple of things.

00:34:09.580 --> 00:34:11.130
I'm just going to
talk through those.

00:34:11.130 --> 00:34:13.960
The first one is
formatted address.

00:34:13.960 --> 00:34:16.210
This is actually a
big challenge to solve

00:34:16.210 --> 00:34:17.909
as a developer,
because if you're

00:34:17.909 --> 00:34:20.949
a delivery or a logistics
company, especially

00:34:20.949 --> 00:34:23.170
a global one, the
way addresses are

00:34:23.170 --> 00:34:26.960
formatted in different parts of
the world varies drastically.

00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:29.642
Some places have something
called a zip code.

00:34:29.642 --> 00:34:30.850
Some places have a post code.

00:34:30.850 --> 00:34:31.780
Some use numbers.

00:34:31.780 --> 00:34:33.370
Some use letters.

00:34:33.370 --> 00:34:37.540
Some put the state before the
zip codes, some put them after.

00:34:37.540 --> 00:34:40.239
And getting that right
can mean the difference

00:34:40.239 --> 00:34:44.080
between your customer receiving
their package on time,

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:48.489
or either not getting it, or
getting it late or not at all.

00:34:48.489 --> 00:34:50.739
And the Places API is able
to format this for you

00:34:50.739 --> 00:34:52.880
exactly to the destination.

00:34:52.880 --> 00:34:54.639
So we handle that
complexity for you.

00:34:54.639 --> 00:34:56.350
We give you the
latitude and longitude.

00:34:56.350 --> 00:34:58.570
We also give you the name.

00:34:58.570 --> 00:35:00.850
But you're also able to get
some additional metadata.

00:35:00.850 --> 00:35:03.220
You can get photos of the place.

00:35:03.220 --> 00:35:07.000
You can get information
like ratings of what

00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:08.500
the users have said about it.

00:35:08.500 --> 00:35:10.665
You can get the
establishment type.

00:35:10.665 --> 00:35:11.290
Is it a school?

00:35:11.290 --> 00:35:11.956
Is it a library?

00:35:11.956 --> 00:35:14.020
Is it a place of worship?

00:35:14.020 --> 00:35:16.200
Is it a bar?

00:35:16.200 --> 00:35:17.970
So there's tons of
great information.

00:35:17.970 --> 00:35:20.111
And if you use the
Places API in your apps,

00:35:20.111 --> 00:35:22.110
the thing to remember is
to hold onto this thing

00:35:22.110 --> 00:35:24.150
at the bottom called
the Place ID, which

00:35:24.150 --> 00:35:27.801
is a unique identifier for
every place in the world.

00:35:27.801 --> 00:35:29.300
Because once you
have that Place ID,

00:35:29.300 --> 00:35:32.090
you can retrieve any
place details you want.

00:35:32.090 --> 00:35:34.410
So it's a great way to
do a local discovery app.

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:36.530
Use the Places API,
do a place search,

00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:38.780
and find what types of
places there are around.

00:35:38.780 --> 00:35:41.540
And you can filter by
establishment types on the web

00:35:41.540 --> 00:35:42.860
servers.

00:35:42.860 --> 00:35:48.200
For the Android and iOS API,
we purpose-built the Places API

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:49.960
for specific use cases.

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:52.296
So rather than searching
anywhere in the world,

00:35:52.296 --> 00:35:54.170
we wanted to make this
a lot more contextual.

00:35:54.170 --> 00:35:57.200
So this is very much about
the places around you.

00:35:57.200 --> 00:36:00.000
So on Android, we call
it getCurrentPlace,

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:02.810
or currentPlaceWithCallback
on iOS.

00:36:02.810 --> 00:36:05.570
And I'm going to show
you a quick demo.

00:36:05.570 --> 00:36:09.260
What happens is when we
use the sensors on a phone,

00:36:09.260 --> 00:36:11.270
we give you a list of
places that the user

00:36:11.270 --> 00:36:13.820
is likely to be at
and some values.

00:36:13.820 --> 00:36:16.820
Now you don't need to see these
values specifically clearly.

00:36:16.820 --> 00:36:18.830
Just know that the
likelihood values are

00:36:18.830 --> 00:36:21.215
going to be between 0 and 1.

00:36:21.215 --> 00:36:23.090
And the closer it is to
1, the more confident

00:36:23.090 --> 00:36:25.070
we are that the user
is at that place.

00:36:25.070 --> 00:36:26.330
So here's a demo.

00:36:26.330 --> 00:36:28.970
We're driving along
the 101, and you'll

00:36:28.970 --> 00:36:32.090
see that the list of places that
we're getting back is changing.

00:36:32.090 --> 00:36:33.436
They're moving up and down.

00:36:33.436 --> 00:36:35.060
And the likelihood
is changing as well,

00:36:35.060 --> 00:36:37.610
as we move along that route.

00:36:37.610 --> 00:36:40.190
And that's where you can
really use this Places API

00:36:40.190 --> 00:36:42.016
to determine where a user is.

00:36:42.016 --> 00:36:43.640
And so in that location
sharing example

00:36:43.640 --> 00:36:47.420
we talked about earlier, I
showed you the Place Picker.

00:36:47.420 --> 00:36:51.350
GetCurrentPlace is the API
that powers the Place Picker.

00:36:51.350 --> 00:36:54.630
So if you want to orchestrate
it yourself, you can do that.

00:36:54.630 --> 00:36:56.370
It's a bit more work for you.

00:36:56.370 --> 00:36:59.510
But it certainly
gives you all the data

00:36:59.510 --> 00:37:03.510
and information you need to
make a good user decision.

00:37:03.510 --> 00:37:06.590
And we've just talked about a
number of web services today.

00:37:06.590 --> 00:37:10.044
They're really, really
useful in different contexts.

00:37:10.044 --> 00:37:11.210
We looked at some use cases.

00:37:11.210 --> 00:37:12.919
We looked at some solutions.

00:37:12.919 --> 00:37:14.960
Now you might find that
you need to use these web

00:37:14.960 --> 00:37:18.530
services on different platforms,
and even though web services

00:37:18.530 --> 00:37:20.390
can be consumed on
any platform, we've

00:37:20.390 --> 00:37:22.910
tried to make that
development easier for you.

00:37:22.910 --> 00:37:25.010
We've built a number
of client libraries.

00:37:25.010 --> 00:37:28.310
And we've built them for
Go, for Python, for NodeJS,

00:37:28.310 --> 00:37:29.399
and for Java.

00:37:29.399 --> 00:37:30.440
And they're open sourced.

00:37:30.440 --> 00:37:34.130
They're available at
github.com/googlemaps.

00:37:34.130 --> 00:37:36.080
And not only do
they make it easier

00:37:36.080 --> 00:37:39.860
to consume these web
services in those languages,

00:37:39.860 --> 00:37:42.790
but we've also put in
some additional features.

00:37:42.790 --> 00:37:45.240
We've made authentication
really, really easy.

00:37:45.240 --> 00:37:50.550
We've added exponential back-off
for when you have any failures.

00:37:50.550 --> 00:37:53.790
We've also made it so that you
can call that API synchronously

00:37:53.790 --> 00:37:57.870
or asynchronously, depending on
whatever style of development

00:37:57.870 --> 00:37:59.225
you are using.

00:37:59.225 --> 00:38:00.600
So it's worth
checking these out.

00:38:03.270 --> 00:38:06.180
So that brings me to the
close of this presentation.

00:38:06.180 --> 00:38:09.450
And we've talked about these
four layers of development--

00:38:09.450 --> 00:38:13.620
delegation, incorporation,
integration, and orchestration.

00:38:13.620 --> 00:38:16.980
We've looked at them in the
context of various use cases

00:38:16.980 --> 00:38:20.540
to build great solutions.

00:38:20.540 --> 00:38:22.880
Everything I've talked about
today is actually available

00:38:22.880 --> 00:38:25.740
on developers.google.com/maps.

00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:28.340
It's where all of our
developer documentation lives.

00:38:28.340 --> 00:38:32.280
And it links out to things like
our GitHub repository as well.

00:38:32.280 --> 00:38:33.710
So please do check it out.

00:38:33.710 --> 00:38:35.360
I want to thank you
for coming today.

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:37.340
And I'm open it up
for Q&amp;A right now.

00:38:37.340 --> 00:38:38.564
Thank you.

00:38:38.564 --> 00:38:43.650
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:45.751
Questions?

00:38:45.751 --> 00:38:47.500
If you do have a
question, if you can just

00:38:47.500 --> 00:38:49.540
come to the microphone, we
have one right in the middle

00:38:49.540 --> 00:38:50.800
here, that would the great.

00:39:02.160 --> 00:39:04.560
AUDIENCE: Ankur, great talk.

00:39:04.560 --> 00:39:08.700
You gave a lot of perspective
on functionalities

00:39:08.700 --> 00:39:10.290
that you're offering
around basically

00:39:10.290 --> 00:39:13.092
location-based services,
navigation, point location.

00:39:13.092 --> 00:39:14.550
I mean, of course,
Google Maps also

00:39:14.550 --> 00:39:17.310
has a very rich collection
of satellite imagery.

00:39:17.310 --> 00:39:20.340
So have you been thinking
to take some of that data

00:39:20.340 --> 00:39:23.667
out and put it through an API?

00:39:23.667 --> 00:39:25.250
ANKUR KOTWAL: Just
the imagery itself?

00:39:25.250 --> 00:39:27.600
AUDIENCE: Yeah, just the
imagery itself, yeah.

00:39:27.600 --> 00:39:30.810
ANKUR KOTWAL: We don't really
do that in a big way today.

00:39:30.810 --> 00:39:36.180
The best-- in the Maps API
specifically, you can get maps

00:39:36.180 --> 00:39:38.340
and you just change the
map style to an image.

00:39:38.340 --> 00:39:41.070
But if you're looking for
map tiles specifically,

00:39:41.070 --> 00:39:43.570
it's not something that
we make readily available

00:39:43.570 --> 00:39:45.614
in this context here.

00:39:48.540 --> 00:39:50.400
If you use some of the
custom styling stuff,

00:39:50.400 --> 00:39:52.800
so it depends on what exactly
what your use case is.

00:39:52.800 --> 00:39:56.610
The closest you could get is
that if you define a custom

00:39:56.610 --> 00:40:01.080
style and turn off all the
labels and things for roads,

00:40:01.080 --> 00:40:07.560
and then set the map
type to satellite,

00:40:07.560 --> 00:40:10.020
you could get a
good satellite view.

00:40:10.020 --> 00:40:12.780
But it really depends on
what you're doing there.

00:40:12.780 --> 00:40:14.287
That might not be sufficient.

00:40:22.080 --> 00:40:25.080
Well, I'm going to
be here on the side

00:40:25.080 --> 00:40:28.150
if anybody has any
follow-up questions.

00:40:28.150 --> 00:40:30.270
The other thing I
